  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's calculate the average rate of change of $f(x)$ over the interval $[a,6]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4(6)-\\frac{6}{6})-(4(a)-\\frac{6}{a})}{6-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-4a+\\frac{6}{a}}{6-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-4a+\\frac{6}{a}}{6-a}\\cdot \\frac{a}{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{23a-4{a}^{2}+6}{a(6-a)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+23a-4{a}^{2}}{a(6-a)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-a)(1+4a)}{a(6-a)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(6-a)}(1+4a)}{a\\cancel{(6-a)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+4a}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}$ be equal to $3$, so \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+4a}{a} & =3 \\\\ 1+4a & =3a \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a<0$, we conclude that $a=-1$ and the interval is $[-1,6].$",
